data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In .NET 10, the Cosmos DB provider allowed synchronous LINQ by opting in to **sync-over-async** (e.g., setting `EnableSyncOverAsync = true` on the Cosmos options). In .NET 11, **that opt-in is removed entirely**. The Cosmos provider now throws `InvalidOperationException` for any synchronous query or save operation.

All four methods in your repository will fail at runtime:

| Method | Failing call |
|--------|-------------|
| `GetAll()` | `ToList()` |
| `GetById()` | `First()` |
| `GetCount()` | `Count()` |
| `Save()` | `SaveChanges()` |

## Fix: convert to async

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- There is no workaround — you must go fully async with the Cosmos provider in .NET 11.
- Callers up the stack (controllers, services) must also be made `async`/`await`.
- The relational providers (SQL Server, PostgreSQL, etc.) still support sync calls, so this is Cosmos-specific.
